# 11.4.3 Bivariate Gamma Degradation Models (Part 3)

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \hat{\sigma}_{i}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left(Y_{i j}^{(i)}-Y_{i j-1}^{(i)}-\hat{\mu}_{i} \Delta t_{j}\right)^{2}}{\sum_{j=1}^{n} \Delta t_{j}-\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left(\Delta t_{j}\right)^{2}}{\sum_{j=1}^{n} \Delta t_{j}}} \text { for } i=1,2 \\
& \hat{\rho}=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left(\Delta Y_{j}^{(1)}-\hat{\mu}_{1} \Delta t_{j}\right)\left(\Delta Y_{j}^{(2)}-\hat{\mu}_{2} \Delta t_{j}\right)}{\sum_{j=1}^{n} \Delta t_{j}-\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left(\Delta t_{j}\right)^{2}}{\sum_{j=1}^{n} \Delta t_{j}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

are unbiased estimators of $\left(\mu_{1}, \mu_{2}, \sigma_{1}^{2}, \sigma_{2}^{2}, \rho\right)$. Now, since we have:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left\{\begin{array}{l}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2} \sigma_{2}^{2} \rho}{\mu_{1} \mu_{2}} \\
b_{i}=\frac{\mu_{i}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}} \text { for } i=1,2 \\
\alpha_{i}=\frac{\mu_{i}^{2}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}-\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2} \sigma_{2}^{2} \rho}{\mu_{1} \mu_{2}} \text { for } i=1,2
\end{array}\right. \\
& \text { we obtain: } \\
& \left\{\begin{array}{l}
\hat{\alpha}_{0}=\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2} \hat{\sigma}_{2}^{2} \hat{\rho}}{\hat{\mu}_{1} \hat{\mu}_{2}} \\
\hat{b}_{i}=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{i}}{\hat{\sigma}_{i}^{2}} \text { for } i=1,2 \\
\hat{\alpha}_{i}=\frac{\hat{\mu}_{i}^{2}}{\hat{\sigma}_{i}^{2}}-\frac{\hat{\sigma}_{1}^{2} \hat{\sigma}_{2}^{2} \hat{\rho}}{\hat{\mu}_{1} \hat{\mu}_{2}} \text { for } i=1,2
\end{array}\right.
\end{aligned}
$$
